def table(rows,widths):
 t=Table([[Paragraph(str(c),styles['Small2']) for c in r] for r in rows],colWidths=widths,hAlign='LEFT');t.setStyle(TableStyle([('BACKGROUND',(0,0),(-1,0),colors.HexColor('#dcebf0')),('VALIGN',(0,0),(-1,-1),'TOP'),('GRID',(0,0),(-1,-1),.4,colors.HexColor('#b8c8ce')),('LEFTPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),9),('RIGHTPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),9),('TOPPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),9),('BOTTOMPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),9)]));story.append(t)
def page(title):
 if story: story.append(PageBreak())
 story.append(Paragraph(title,styles['Title']));story.append(Spacer(1,16))
page('Programming Finance<br/>Project Report #1')
p('<b>Conceptual design | Fall 2026 | October 18, 2026</b>')
p('Submission metadata: team name and member names have not been supplied. Add these before submission. This report describes the proposed system; implementation and verification are documented separately.')
h('Purpose and intended users')
p('The proposed server-side application helps individuals with modest investing knowledge compare explainable stock strategies, manage a simulated portfolio of approximately 20 companies, and understand how coordinated trades change portfolio risk. It supports investment horizons of 1, 2, and 5 years. Its emphasis is software engineering and transparent evaluation, rather than a claim of profitable investment advice.')
h('Questions the system answers')
p('What do historical price trends suggest for my portfolio? How often should I review its allocation? Which combination of purchases and sales would reduce estimated variance within a chosen historical window? What happened after a recorded recommendation? Can my simulated orders be aggregated with orders from other users without sharing their cash?')
h('Scope and design basis')
p('The supplied four-page assignment is the authoritative course specification. The additional user prompt requests a complete implementation after this conceptual design. Lecture #1 slides 13 and 17 were not supplied and have not been reviewed; this design uses the assignment and the established sources listed on page 7.')
p('A REST API and console output are sufficient. MySQL stores persistent state; Jasmine tests verify behavior. There is no custom client interface, real brokerage connection, margin, short selling, or real-money execution. Basket trading is adopted as the optional assignment enhancement requested in the user prompt.')
page('1 | A minimal financial vocabulary')
p('Five familiar concepts form the user-facing model. Investment horizon is an attribute of a portfolio; holdings and cash are portfolio state. Technical analysis and risk are measurements used by a strategy, rather than extra accounts or instruments users must manage.')
h('Stock')
p('A stock represents an ownership interest in a company. The application identifies each stock by a symbol and uses dated prices to measure changes in value; holding a stock does not guarantee a positive return. [1]')
h('Portfolio')
p('A portfolio is the collection of investments held for an investor. Here it contains stock positions and simulated cash, with a chosen time horizon; allocation describes how its value is distributed among those positions. [2]')
h('Trading strategy')
p('A trading strategy is a repeatable set of rules for deciding when and how to trade. This system uses technical indicators derived from historical prices to make its rules inspectable, while recognizing that those indicators cannot establish future performance. [3]')
h('Order')
p('An order is an instruction to buy or sell a specified security. Market orders seek execution at an available market price, but an observed quote does not guarantee the price available at execution; this project instead uses an explicitly labeled price-taking simulator. [4]')
h('Basket trade')
p('A basket trade coordinates multiple securities as a group. The project extends that idea with an operational aggregation batch that groups compatible simulated orders from several users while retaining separate beneficial ownership. [5]')
h('Why these five are sufficient')
p('Stocks identify what can be traded; portfolios identify ownership, resources, and goals; strategies explain recommendations; orders express user decisions; baskets coordinate them. Quotes, recommendations, fills, and evaluations are supporting records of these concepts. User identity, data providers, AI adapters, and database transactions are implementation services, not additional financial concepts.')
page('2 | Essential state and operations')
table([['Concept','Minimum state','Operations and purpose'],['Stock','Symbol; currency; dated raw quotes; adjusted historical prices; source and coverage','Inspect quote and history; refresh observations. Source and time make evidence auditable.'],['Portfolio','Owner; cash; whole-share holdings; horizon (1/2/5 years)','Fund simulation; inspect value; choose horizon; request analysis; approve rebalance. Ownership separates users.'],['Trading strategy','Indicator windows; review interval; allocation constraints; cutoff; recorded recommendation','Analyze; propose target weights and trades; evaluate later outcomes. Cutoff prevents future data entering a decision.'],['Order','Owner portfolio; symbol; buy/sell; integer quantity; state; request key; timestamps','Submit; cancel while pending; execute or reject. Stable keys prevent duplicate fills after retries.'],['Basket trade','Batch key; cutoff; member orders; symbol/side groups; fills and allocations','Collect eligible orders; validate; execute; reconcile. Every allocation retains its original owner.']],[76,190,238])
h('Derived records and invariants')
p('A holding records how many shares a portfolio owns. An execution records the price and quantity actually simulated, and a recommendation records the inputs, rules, and suggested actions at a particular time. An evaluation connects that recommendation to subsequent observations.')
p('Cash and holdings must remain nonnegative. No sale can exceed owned shares, and no purchase can spend another user\'s money. A committed fill updates cash, shares, order status, and audit records together; an aborted transaction changes none of them.')
p('The proposed implementation rounds USD cash to cents using decimal half-up rounding and accepts whole shares only. Adjusted historical series support returns; raw quotes price simulated orders. These series are never silently substituted for one another.')
page('3 | Relationships and action effects')
d=Drawing(504,230)
boxes=[(8,150,'Portfolio'),(345,150,'Stock'),(8,35,'Strategy'),(180,95,'Order'),(345,35,'Basket trade')]
for x,y,label in boxes:
 d.add(Rect(x,y,150,45,fillColor=colors.HexColor('#e8f1f5'),strokeColor=colors.HexColor('#52788a')));d.add(String(x+12,y+18,label,fontSize=11))
for x1,y1,x2,y2 in [(158,172,345,172),(83,150,83,80),(158,150,230,140),(330,118,420,150),(330,110,420,80)]: d.add(Line(x1,y1,x2,y2,strokeColor=colors.HexColor('#52788a')))
d.add(String(190,183,'holds 0..20',fontSize=9));d.add(String(90,115,'analyzes',fontSize=9));d.add(String(195,65,'groups orders',fontSize=9));story.append(d)
p('<b>Cardinalities.</b> One user owns many portfolios. Each portfolio holds zero or more stocks and can submit many orders; each order refers to exactly one stock and one portfolio. A basket contains multiple orders, each with a traceable fill allocation. A strategy produces many dated recommendations for portfolios.')
h('Analysis does not execute')
p('Requesting analysis reads a portfolio and historical observations, computes indicators and covariance, and stores a recommendation. It does not change cash or holdings. The user must explicitly approve a proposed trade set. Optional AI text explains computed results and is labeled separately from numeric evidence.')
h('Execution changes related concepts atomically')
p('Approving a coordinated rebalance validates all legs at one quote snapshot, sells first within the transaction, then buys using only that portfolio\'s cash. Either all legs commit or all roll back. The proposal is revalidated because holdings and prices may have changed since analysis.')
h('Aggregation is distinct from portfolio coordination')
p('A multi-user batch groups pending orders by symbol and side. It uses one price per group and full fills only, preserving order-level allocations; buys and sells are not netted. An unaffordable order is rejected without borrowing from another portfolio. Cancellation and execution compete under locks, so an order cannot both cancel and fill.')
page('4 | Concrete use cases')
table([['Use case','Interaction and observable result'],['Set a goal','A user creates a portfolio, funds simulated cash, and chooses 1, 2, or 5 years. Invalid horizons are rejected.'],['Inspect evidence','The user requests history for up to 20 years. The response shows available coverage, source, and missing history; recent IPOs are not given invented observations.'],['Ask for a strategy','For 20 selected stocks, the service reports trends, RSI, return and volatility over stated intervals. It proposes monthly, quarterly, or semiannual reviews according to horizon.'],['Reduce estimated risk','The user compares existing, equal-weight, and constrained minimum-variance allocations using the same aligned observations. The system reports infeasibility or insufficient shared history explicitly.'],['Approve coupled trades','The user approves whole-share sell and buy legs. Execution rechecks current cash and quotes; residual cash and each fill are displayed. Failure rolls back the complete rebalance.'],['Join a batch','Two users submit buys of the same stock. A simulator batch prices them together, allocates each entire order, and records separate cash debits and holdings.'],['Cancel or reject','A pending order may be cancelled. Stale quotes, insufficient cash, and insufficient shares produce explicit errors or recorded rejections without inconsistent balances.'],['Evaluate a decision','The user requests performance after the recommendation cutoff. The service excludes earlier observations and reports measured outcomes alongside baselines and assumptions.']],[108,396])
h('An independently checkable example')
p('At a simulated quote of $12.50, an account with $100 buys 3 shares: cost $37.50, ending cash $62.50, and holdings increase by 3. A second user buying 2 shares pays $25.00 from their own cash. The aggregate is 5 shares and $62.50, exactly equal to the two allocations; repeating the same request must not debit either user twice.')
page('5 | Analysis, integrations, and boundaries')
h('Explainable analysis')
p('The initial strategy uses moving averages for trend, 14-observation RSI for momentum, and historical return, volatility, correlation, and drawdown for context. It compares trailing 1-, 2-, 5-, 10-, and 20-year intervals when available. The future investment horizon is distinct from these past observation windows.')
p('A long-only minimum-variance proposal uses aligned returns, a covariance estimate, and a concentration cap. It compares estimated risk against existing holdings and equal weights over the identical window. Constraints, sample size, regularization, and convergence are reported. Diversification can reduce some risks but cannot guarantee protection against losses. [2]')
h('External information and AI boundary')
p('A replaceable market-data adapter requests daily history and quotes. Alpha Vantage documents adjusted historical data, quotes, and news endpoints; full adjusted history requires appropriate provider entitlement. Data source, observation time, retrieval time, currency, and adjustment status accompany stored prices. [6]')
p('An offline provider generates deterministic synthetic prices solely for tests and demonstrations. It never claims to be actual market history. News and AI interfaces are optional and credential-based; external text is untrusted. AI may produce a separate explanation but cannot change calculated signals or authorize orders.')
h('Evaluation and limitations')
p('Historical evaluation makes decisions from observations available at time T and executes no earlier than the next observation. Forward evaluation records a decision first and waits for later data. Both must identify timing, costs, slippage, benchmarks, and coverage. Historical replay is not evidence of future live success.')
p('USD equities, whole shares, zero simulator fees, full fills, and no exchange market impact keep the engineering scope manageable. The simulator does not reproduce live liquidity, settlement, corporate-action processing on held shares, or tax accounting. Adjusted data may be revised; point-in-time corporate-action history is outside this prototype, so retrospective results require that caveat.')
page('6 | References and actual AI assistance')
p('The following pages were opened or retrieved through web search on October 18, 2026. Definitions above are concise paraphrases. These sources establish terminology and provider capabilities; the proposed rules and system boundaries are design decisions.')
refs=[('1','SEC Investor.gov, Stocks - FAQs','https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/stocks'),('2','SEC Investor.gov, Asset Allocation and Diversification','https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/getting-started/asset-allocation'),('3','Fidelity, Technical Analysis Indicator Guide','https://www.fidelity.com/learning-center/trading-investing/technical-analysis/technical-indicator-guide/overview'),('4','SEC Investor.gov, Types of Orders','https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/how-stock-markets-work/types-orders'),('5','Investopedia, Basket Trading Essentials','https://www.investopedia.com/terms/b/baskettrade.asp'),('6','Alpha Vantage, API Documentation','https://www.alphavantage.co/documentation/')]
for n,title,url in refs: story.append(Paragraph(f'[{n}] <b>{title}</b><br/><link href="{url}" color="#176585">{url}</link>',styles['Small2']))
h('How I directed the use of OpenAI')
p('I supplied OpenAI Codex with the assignment PDF and a detailed, structured prompt listing the project requirements and how I wanted the work carried out. I instructed it to read the entire PDF first, treat it as the authoritative specification, and complete the work through design, implementation, testing, debugging, documentation, and packaging. In a follow-up message, I emphasized that the pasted prompt should guide the work.')
p('For the conceptual design, my prompt required a separate 5-10-page Report #1, a minimal set of established financial concepts, two- or three-sentence definitions with verified references, and essential attributes and operations. I also specified concrete use cases, relationships, effects of actions across concepts, explicit assumptions, and a clear distinction between financial concepts and implementation services. I instructed the AI not to invent missing lecture content, team names, or course details.')
p('For the subsequent implementation, I specified a server-side Node.js/TypeScript application with Express, MySQL, Jasmine tests, and reproducible setup. My instructions explicitly covered 1-, 2-, and 5-year horizons, approximately 20 companies, up to 20 years of price history, technical-analysis recommendations, coordinated risk-aware trades, multi-user basket trading, external market information, and an AI-agent interface. I required simulated execution, precise money calculations, ownership checks, and transaction consistency.')
p('I also directed the AI to test boundary cases, fix discovered failures, evaluate recommendations only against later observations, and preserve actual prompts, code versions, problems, fixes, and verification results. The original structured prompt is retained in the project evidence folder. These instructions guided the AI-assisted work; the separate development log records the implementation and testing details.')

